Structure vs Union in C++This repository demonstrates the core concepts, differences,
and practical code implementations of Structures and Unions in C++.📦 

1. Structure
(struct)DefinitionA Structure is a user-defined data type that groups related variables
of different data types (like int, float, char) under a single name. In a structure, 
every member gets its own unique memory space

.Where is it used?
To model real-world objects that have multiple attributes (e.g., a Student with a roll 
number, name, and marks).Creating game entities (e.g., a Player with x-coordinate,
y-coordinate, and health).Handling database records where each row has different columns.🤝



2. Union (union)DefinitionA 
Union is also a user-defined data type that groups different variables together. However,
all members share the exact same memory location. The size of the union is equal to the size
of its largest member. You can only store a value in one member at a time.

Where is it used?
Memory-constrained systems like embedded devices, microcontrollers, and IoT hardware 
where saving RAM is critical.Implementing hardware registers where a single byte of memory 
needs to be read in different formats.Creating polymorphic types where a variable 
needs to hold only one type of data at any given moment.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

----structure-----

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

struct Student {
    int roll;
    float marks;
};

int main() {
    Student s1;

    cout << "--- Enter Structure Data ---" << endl;
    cout << "Enter Roll Number: ";
    cin >> s1.roll;
    cout << "Enter Marks: ";
    cin >> s1.marks;

    cout << endl << "--- Structure Output ---" << endl;
    cout << "Roll No: " << s1.roll << endl;
    cout << "Marks: " << s1.marks << endl;

    return 0;
}


